Hull-Weave

Ingredients: PROGRESSIVE METAL + POWER METAL + COZY, WARM & CHILL LO-FI + Piano  
Creative Catalyst: Its first record was cut aboard a generation ship halfway to Proxima Centauri.

Hull-Weave sounds like an impossible midnight lullaby broadcast across light-years—a clash where towering, triumphant power metal vocal soaring and complex, shifting progressive time signatures are stripped of their aggressive distortion and smothered in velvet. The electric guitars are replaced by an upright piano taped with felt and micro-contact mics, delivering warm, dusty chords that slide through 13/8 meters, while heavy metal drum fills are played with soft wire brushes on recycled metal canisters. It is triumph without volume, epic heroism played at the volume of a quiet room where everyone else is sleeping.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 65–85 BPM, featuring metric modulation where hyper-speed double-bass drum patterns are slowed down to a lazy, swinging lo-fi shuffle while retaining complex polyrhythmic accents.
- Harmonic Palette: Soaring Lydian and Dorian modes typical of power metal, transposed onto rich, suspended lo-fi jazz chords (Maj9, min11) played on muted piano keys.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Sub-Hull Resonances: Capturing the low-frequency mechanical hum of a vessel's life-support systems and sidechaining it to the kick drum.
  2. Cassette Freeze-Thaw: Passing rapid-fire progressive metal arpeggios through a worn tape loop that intentionally warps the pitch down a semitone as it fades.
  3. Micro-Felt Dampening: Inserting strips of recycled thermal flight-suit wool directly onto the piano strings to kill high-end harshness, rendering fast metal runs into soft, rhythmic raindrops.
- Dynamics: Starts as an intimate, crackling bedroom piano solo, slowly layering sweeping polyphonic vocals and warm brassy synths into an overwhelming, cozy crescendo that never breaks the room's hushed volume limit.

